    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Civic engagement, as defined by Stanford University's Professor Thomas Ehrlich, encompasses efforts to improve community life through various processes, including both political and non-political actions. Despite a decline in civic education offerings in schools nationwide, alternative methods like Youth Participatory Action Research (YPAR) nurture civic skills among young people that extend beyond the classroom. Through the research process, YPAR provides a positive youth development experience that supports the development of youth capacity in the areas of communication and collaboration, while building critical consciousness. By building these capacities, youth enhance their understanding of their community and their power to make a difference. This policy brief highlights the importance of incorporating youth voices into program improvement and policymaking. Giving students the opportunity to co-create with adults will prepare them for civic life. This will support youth today and their civic engagement in the future, which will benefit everyone, ultimately benefiting society as a whole.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Aguilar, Y. (2024). The Importance of Youth Voice and Civic Engagement: Youth Participatory Action Research (YPAR). Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/my785xr4262. https://doi.org/10.25740/my785xr4262.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
